    {
      "id": "GENUS_MYRMECOPHAGA",
      "name": "Myrmecophaga",
      "rank": "GENUS",
      "description": "The giant anteater — the largest of all anteaters and one of the most recognisable animals of South America. It can be 2.1 m long from nose to tail and weigh 35 kg. The giant anteater has no teeth; its stomach has a modified muscular lining that grinds insects like a gizzard. Its tongue is 60 cm long, covered in sticky saliva, and can flick in and out up to 150 times per minute. A single anteater may visit over 200 termite and ant nests per day, consuming up to 35,000 insects. It never destroys a nest completely — withdrawing before the insects mount an effective defence, allowing colonies to recover for future visits. Its massive, hooked forelaws are primarily used for tearing open nests and are so large that the animal walks on its knuckles.",
      "lineage": "Giant Anteater",
      "children": [
        {
